    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Emotional Chromatic Number of Social Networks\n\nThe emotional chromatic number `emoChrom G = min { k \u2265 3 : G is k-colorable }` is the smallest\nnumber of emotion categories (at least three) needed to assign emotions to people so that no two\nfriends share one. The current cycle establishes `emoChrom(K_n) = max(n, 3)`, `emoChrom(C_n) = 3`\nfor `n \u2265 3`, the six-emotion window `3 \u2264 emoChrom \u2264 6` for 6-colorable networks, and refutes the\nfolklore \"bipartite root at k = 2.\" The following conjectures push the invariant further.\n\n## 1. The emotional floor is the only correction: `emoChrom = max(\u03c7, 3)`\n\n**Conjecture.** For every finite graph `G`, `emoChrom G = max(\u03c7(G), 3)`, so the emotional chromatic\nnumber is determined by the ordinary chromatic number and a universal floor.\n\n**The key insight is** that colorability is monotone in the number of colors, so the set of admissible\nemotion counts is an interval `[max(\u03c7, 3), \u221e)` whose least element is `max(\u03c7, 3)`; the emotional\nconstraint contributes exactly one thing \u2014 the floor at three.\n\n**Why now?** The clique and cycle computations already exhibit both regimes (`\u03c7 \u2265 3` cliques track\n`\u03c7`, while `\u03c7 = 2` bipartite graphs are lifted to `3`), so a single uniform identity is the natural\nconsolidation and would make every downstream emotional statistic a corollary of chromatic theory.\n\n## 2. Emotional monotonicity under friendship growth\n\n**Conjecture.** Adding a friendship (an edge) to a social network can raise the emotional chromatic\nnumber by at most one, and removing one can lower it by at most one; consequently networks that\ndiffer in a single friendship have emotional chromatic numbers differing by at most one.\n\n**The key insight is** that a proper coloring of the larger graph restricts to the smaller one, while\na coloring of the smaller graph extends across a new edge after recoloring at most one endpoint, so\nthe invariant is 1-Lipschitz with respect to edge edits.\n\n**Why now?** Deletion\u2013contraction (already formalized for the chromatic polynomial in this catalog)\ngives exact control of how coloring counts change under edge edits; lifting that control from counts\nto the floored minimum `emoChrom` is the immediate next step.\n\n## 3. Sparse networks stay inside the six-emotion window\n\n**Conjecture.** Every social network in which each person has at most five friends satisfies\n`emoChrom G \u2264 6`; more generally, bounded-degree networks have emotional chromatic number bounded by\none more than the maximum number of friends, capped below by three.\n\n**The key insight is** that a greedy assignment, processing people one at a time, always finds a free\nemotion whenever the number of already-colored friends is below the palette size, so `deg_max + 1`\nemotions always suffice.\n\n**Why now?** Real friendship networks are overwhelmingly sparse, so a degree-based bound explains the\nempirical observation that emotional chromatic numbers cluster in `[3, 6]`, turning the six-emotion\nwindow from a hypothesis into a structural guarantee for realistic networks.\n\n## 4. Planarity forces at most six emotions\n\n**Conjecture.** Every social network that can be drawn without crossing friendships (a planar\nnetwork) has emotional chromatic number in `{3, 4}`, and never needs more than four emotions.\n\n**The key insight is** that planar graphs are four-colorable, so the floor and the four-color bound\nsqueeze the invariant into a two-value range, independent of network size.\n\n**Why now?** Many geographically embedded social networks are approximately planar, so a sharp\ntwo-value classification would give an exact emotional taxonomy for an entire, practically relevant\nfamily of networks.\n\n## 5. The emotional floor detects triangle-free structure\n\n**Conjecture.** Among triangle-free social networks, `emoChrom G = 3` precisely captures the\nnon-bipartite ones together with the trivial floor: a triangle-free network has emotional chromatic\nnumber `3` iff it is either non-bipartite (contains an odd friendship cycle) or has at least one\nfriendship, and there exist triangle-free networks with arbitrarily large ordinary chromatic number\nyet whose emotional profile is dominated by the floor only until \u03c7 overtakes it.\n\n**The key insight is** that the emotional floor masks the fine distinction between \u03c7 = 2 and \u03c7 = 3,\nso the invariant becomes a coarse odd-cycle detector until chromatic number climbs past three.\n\n**Why now?** Triangle-free graphs with large chromatic number (Mycielski-type constructions) are a\nclassical source of surprises; testing how the emotional floor interacts with them probes exactly\nwhere the invariant stops being a simple floor and starts tracking deep chromatic structure.\n",
